[Music]  so  [Music]  hello my name is cecil slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songs from testimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slupovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii we will talk about the polish jewish  songs that were sung by peret's h  a holocaust survivor born in 1927  in warsaw when war broke out he was a  little boy  parrots used to sing in the streets of  his home city to earn  a living and shared several songs in his  1993  fortunate video archive testimony songs  of the survivors  like parrots are of truly special value  as they shed a unique light on the  polish and more specifically  warsaw music culture on the interwar and  world war  ii periods some of the texts like this  one in parity's testimony are not to be  found  in any known sources today pochongo  yestwok  crowded train is one of at least two  polish renditions of the popular mexican  coppola  song sielita lindo just like val's  francois  that we discussed in the first episode  this is the song that paris h  sang in the courtyards of warsaw along  with many other children  like himself as well as local street  bands  which would have been lost to history if  not for the testimony of parrot's age  refers to the depression and smuggling  of rosaries  in the pre-war warsaw in post-war  poland the song gained newfound  popularity  thanks in part to leonard buchkovsky  1946 movie zakazane piosenki  forbidden songs the new version although  similar to the pre-war version  sung by parrots referred to the war  terrazie esteboina  tengem it is war now those who sell  live decades later in 1982  the polish cielito lindo came back to  life with a new lyrics  yet again in the wake of the military  coup d'etat  is  yes  okay  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  is  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  is  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  is hmm  crowded train it is a very special  song which help people might say living  even survive but  the origin of this song is not in polish  language  and in not in polish culture the origins  comes to  back to latin america  mexico where it was called  chilita lindo and it was a  folk song belonging to the cop  and performed by  mariachi wandering bands during that  time and it was popularized  by the composer quirino  in late 19th century he used this  melody for his composition  the the wrestler and from that time it  became enormously popular  in mexico in different spanish culture  diasporas  in the world but very soon also  in other cultures as well so  the song was performed by such famous  singers  like placido domingo luciano pavarotti  in poland by famous singers  violeta villas or maria  who performed it under the title  long-living double dancing party which  became a very popular  song during communist time in poland  but it came to poland  in the 30s of last century  via berlin  and it was sung by a  famous chilean singer  at that time  rosita serrano who came  to berlin singing  it she was called julian  nightingale very famous singer and that  that was uh the way how how it came from  the  berlin to pre-war were so it was time of  depression  a time of economical crisis so  the lyrics to this song  were in polish were about  smuggling groceries  in trains or trams in warsaw  and this version of the song was  forgotten  in in poland and i think thanks to the  performance  by mr parrots in that recording  we still can have this lyric since his  first version of polish  presence of of this song at  that time we may call this type of songs  when we called  which means that there is a substitution  of one text to the another  without any substantial change in music  so we keep  the melody and change the lyrics  according to circumstances  historical political cultural  circumstances  so when war came  towards so uh to poland in  1939 the lyrics  changed and it was  mostly about the war kerashi's voice  it is war now who sells  leaves and  it was sang by the people on the streets  who from one side like mr parrots  wanted to survive to make their living  from the other hand it was sang  by people who were engaged in  underground movement  so it was still about  smuggling but not necessarily groceries  also weapons and illegal literature as  well  and because of that it was  sometimes very dangerous to be a singer  of that songs  because many of them belong to the  underground  movement and took part in actions again  against germans during the occupation  time so we started to call  that songs forbidden songs and this is a  title  of a famous movie which was  icing the first feature movie after the  war in poland  created in 1947  by the film director a film director  leonard buchkowski who established his  career before the war as director of  famous war movies  with big budget  and and he cooperated  after the war with ludwig starsky for  screenwriting who was an author of  screen  writing for this movie ludwig starsky  was the father of a famous set designer  film  designer alan starsky who worked for  many years with sanjivada  and he was also awarded by  academy award for schindler  schindler's list in 1994  so they both buchkovsky star  estarski decided to make a song  a hero of this film  so it is a plot of course around  it about different underground  activities in  in in warsaw during the occupation  but the main hero of this movie  is a song the forgotten songs and among  them the crowded train  the communist authorities were not  satisfied with  the first version of this movie it was  not enough  for them politically engaged on the  soviet  uh side and not enough  underlying the cruelty of  german soldiers so  after two years they made a second  version  of of this movie and  you know it it it became a very  a very famous  film which popular popularizes  song crowded train again  but this is not end of the story  and this song has long living  in fact when we in poland had a  so-called martial law uh implemented by  general  jaroszewski in december  13 1981  uh mostly people in poland felt that  there is another type of occupation that  the war came back it was a war we see in  our society  but it was a good ground a new context  for  for such songs from the world time  and again crowded train became  very popular at that time under  of course a new title  and with new lyrics now about  green crow jealous verona verona was in  the aberration  of government name  you know in this new regime  circumstances  so it was a new living for  that song and i wish  that it will be not the end  of the story uh i wish  that this song will survive  in our cultural memory in archives  and that this song will influence  new productions and performings  but my hope is that never again  in connection with new wars in the  future let's keep it  for the peace time  [Music]  you

